 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
-- Since: 2026-09-11
-- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
-- Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
-- Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
-  the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+### §check-fails-closed — an internal error in the check aborts it
+- Since: 2026-10-22 (replaces §check-fails-open, 2026-09-11)
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit, which blocks the commit.
+  Large staged files are still only a warning: when nothing fails, the check exits 0. The file loop runs in the main
+  shell, not inside `$(...)`, because bash does not apply `-e` inside command substitution (macOS bash 3.2 has no
+  `inherit_errexit`).
+- Forbidden: removing `-e` / `errexit`; hiding a failure to keep the check passing (`|| true`, `2>/dev/null`); a bare
+  `cond && cmd` as the last command of a loop body, which returns non-zero when `cond` is false and aborts the check
+  on ordinary input.
+- Rejected: fail-open (§check-fails-open, 2026-09-11 to 2026-10-22: no `-e`, always `exit 0`); overridden by the
+  maintainer on 2026-10-22.
+- Reason: maintainer decision — a failing command should stop the check, not be passed over silently. The 2026-09-10
+  incident under the old `set -e` (a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file blocked every commit for a day) is
+  now prevented by skipping paths that are not regular files (`[ -f "$f" ] || continue`), not by ignoring errors.
+  Keep that guard.
